  "query_preview": "..(Thursday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 510 PM EDT TUE SEP 8 2026\n\nThe models are in generally good agreement for the majority of \nthe extended portion of the forecast. Ridging starts out over the \nsoutheastern CONUS as well as the Desert Southwest, while faster \nand progressive flow dominates across the northern half of the \nCONUS. A notable short wave trough will be aligned from the Upper \nMidwest through the middle Mississippi Valley. This feature will \ncruise through the Ohio Valley and eventually New England by \nFriday. Troughing will be maintained across the Mississippi \nValley Friday into Saturday, as additional short wave energy moves\nalong the north central CONUS. Meanwhile, a deeper upper level \nlow will propagate onshore over the Pacific Northwest. As this \nfeature swings east, more prominent ridging will take hold \ndownstream across the southeastern CONUS late this weekend and \ninto early next week. \n\nSensible weather will feature an active start across eastern\nKentucky for Thursday. A cold front will approach from the \nnorthwest, with more widespread convection moving in from \nnorthwest to southeast during the day. The highest probabilities\nwill occur north KY-80, with perhaps a slightly slower trend seen\nin the latest model guidance. PWATs will peak in the 1.8 to 1.9 \ninch range, along with the surface boundary aligning more parallel\nwith the mid and upper level flow. As such, some locally heavy \nrainers remain a possibility. Highs on Thursday will be in the mid\nto upper 80s. Showers and thunderstorms will continue to threaten\nthrough Thursday evening, although as instability wanes and the \noverall forcing diminish, convective coverage should gradually \nlessen overnight. Lows Thursday night will range from the mid 60s \nnorth of I-64, to the upper 60s bordering Tennessee. These lows \nwound up being just a shade higher than the blended guidance, \ngiven the tendency of the cold front to hang up as it moves across\nour region. \n\nThis boundary will linger across our southern locales on Friday, \nwith a resurgence of good chance to likely PoPs seen generally \nalong and southeast of KY-80. Highs will retreat closer to normal \ntemporarily, mainly in the low to mid 80s. We will still be under\nthe influence of troughing aloft on Saturday, although some subtle \n500 mb height rises may actually occur. Still, enough low level\nmoisture will linger to allow for additional diurnally-driven \nconvection. Similar to Friday, higher PoPs will be favored across\nour southeast. From Sunday through Tuesday, ridging will be \nbuilding in aloft, keeping convective coverage more limited. \nUnfortunately, this will also mean a continuation of above normal \nheat and humidity across eastern Kentucky, with highs averaging in\nthe 85 to 90 degree range each day, and dew points remaining \nabove 70 degrees at times.",
